00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:06.470 align:start position:0%
the deposed president of guinea no  longer a free man but under house arrest  after returning home from exile the once  feared leader seen as untouchable and  above the law is facing justice guinea's  attorney general ordered legal  proceedings against alfa conde and 26  former members of his government they're  accused of murder torture sexual  violence and the destruction of public  property conde's party members say it's  a political witch hunt  the rpg and its allies demand the  respect of the principle of presumption  of innocence and the depolitisation of  the current procedure consider  suspending its participation in the  national meetings recall the need to  appoint an international mediator in the  guinean crisis  it was only after conde was deposed in a  coup last september that the  investigation was opened for crimes  committed during his time in office  security forces  fired bullets at protesters in the  run-up to conde's contested re-election  in 2020. the 84-year-old changed the  constitution allowing him to run for a  third term  rights groups say at least 40 protesters  were killed and around 200 injured in  the months leading up to the election  guinea's military leader general mamadi  dombuya fired all 40 generals accused of  involvement in the killings he promises  a corruption cleanup saying the powerful  will face justice  but in a tv address on sunday he pointed  out that the free and fair elections  won't happen for another three years  while visiting west africa this week the  un secretary general called on guinea's  leader to hand over power to a civilian  administration immediately  the regional body ecowas has imposed  sanctions because of the election delay  dumboya's rule is seen by his critics as  a step back for democracy but conde's  arrest is being seen as a step forward  for justice in a country where impunity  for the powerful has long been the rule  nicholas hawk al jazeera
 

